(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-i-quiz"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies I Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which group with acute myeloid leukemia has the lowest five-year survival?","choices":["Favorable-risk patients","Younger patients","Promyelocytic survivors","Patients over 70"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Survival is lowest for patients over 70, only 5% at five years, and for adverse-risk or secondary disease, about 10% overall. It is highest, apart from acute promyelocytic leukemia, for favorable-risk disease and younger patients. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 41, 43"},{"q":"What defines myelodysplastic syndromes?","choices":["Dysplastic myeloid cells","Mature cell overproduction","Fibrosis of the marrow","Blasts of 20% or more"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myelodysplastic syndromes are a group of myeloid malignancies defined by dysplasia (abnormal appearance) of myeloid cells, with distorted hematopoiesis, immune dysregulation and genomic changes. They present as cytopenias, most often anemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 45, 47, 51"},{"q":"How is blast-phase chronic myelogenous leukemia treated?","choices":["Observation with blood counts","Stopping therapy for remission","Acute myeloid leukemia-like regimens","A tyrosine kinase inhibitor by itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blast phase, or blast crisis, behaves as an acute leukemia, usually like acute myeloid leukemia, often with unique chromosomal and mutational changes; it requires acute myeloid leukemia-like regimens and responds poorly to tyrosine kinase inhibitors. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 62, 63, 65"},{"q":"Why do patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia have bone or joint pain?","choices":["Fibrosis of the marrow","Thick red cell blood","Clots from high platelets","Marrow overproduction of blasts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bone or joint pain in acute lymphoblastic leukemia is due to very excessive marrow production of leukemia cells. It sits alongside anemia, bleeding, infections, B-symptoms and, in more than half, lymphadenopathy, hepatomegaly or splenomegaly. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which cytopenia is most frequent in myelodysplastic syndrome?","choices":["Lymphocytosis","Anemia","Neutropenia","Thrombocytopenia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Myelodysplastic syndrome presents most frequently with anemia, causing fatigue, weakness and pale skin; neutropenia and occasional thrombocytopenia also occur. Low-risk disease is commonly defined by anemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"A patient manages limited self-care and spends more than 50% of waking hours in a bed or chair. What is the ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance grade?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Capable of limited self-care and confined to bed or chair more than 50% of waking hours is grade 3. Grade 2 is up and about more than half the day with all self-care; grade 4 cannot carry on any self-care. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the goal of treatment in lower-risk myelodysplastic syndrome?","choices":["Improve quality of life","Cure by transplant","Remission with 7+3","Major molecular response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lower-risk myelodysplastic syndrome has a median survival above 5 years and very low risk of acute myeloid leukemia, but patients often have complications from anemia, bleeding or infections, so the goal of treatment is to improve quality of life. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"About what share of all lymphomas are non-Hodgkin lymphomas?","choices":["10%","50%","70%","90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma accounts for about 90% of all lymphomas and includes both B-cell and T-cell lymphomas. Hodgkin lymphoma, with its Reed-Sternberg cells, makes up the remaining 10%. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which clotting complications are named for essential thrombocythemia?","choices":["Leukemic transformation","Stroke and heart attack","Portal hypertension","Disseminated coagulopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Complications of essential thrombocythemia are clotting, including stroke (cerebrovascular accident) and heart attack (myocardial infarction). Presentation may also be headaches, fatigue, paresthesias or vision changes from clotting. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"At 12 months of treatment for chronic myelogenous leukemia, the BCR-ABL1 (breakpoint cluster region-Abelson 1) level is 0.05%. Which milestone category is this?","choices":["Failure","Warning","Optimal","Treatment-free remission"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At 12 months, the table reads failure above 1%, warning 0.1-1% and optimal below 0.1%. A level of 0.05% is below 0.1%, so the response is optimal; treatment-free remission is not applicable at 12 months. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Polycythemia vera is overproduction of which cells?","choices":["Red blood cells","Platelets","Granulocytes","Plasma cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myeloproliferative neoplasms are rare myeloid malignancies with atypical proliferation of mature cell lines; polycythemia vera is over-production of red blood cells, which increases blood viscosity. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 51, 54, 56, 16"},{"q":"Which cell feature defines an acute leukemia?","choices":["Myeloid cells proliferate","Lymph nodes are involved","Immature cells proliferate","Mature cells proliferate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute leukemias arise from more immature, progenitor white blood cells and cause rapid proliferation of that clone. Chronic leukemias involve proliferation of mature malignant white blood cells. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 14, 7"},{"q":"If a patient survives the initial presentation of acute promyelocytic leukemia, what is the cure rate?","choices":["About 10%","About 25%","About 50%","Over 90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute promyelocytic leukemia has high early mortality from disseminated intravascular coagulation and bleeding, but if the patient survives the initial presentation, the prognosis is excellent, with a cure rate above 90%. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 41, 43"},{"q":"What sorts acute myeloid leukemia into favorable, intermediate and poor risk?","choices":["Cytogenetics and mutations","The presenting white count","The blast percentage","The performance grade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute myeloid leukemia shows tremendous variety in cytogenetic abnormalities and mutations, and risk is broken down into favorable, intermediate and poor on that basis. Cytogenetic and mutation analysis is important for prognosis. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 32, 38"},{"q":"In chronic myelogenous leukemia, a marrow with about 2% blasts fits which phase?","choices":["Acute myeloid leukemia","Chronic phase","Accelerated phase","Blast phase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The chronic (stable) phase is the early proliferative phase with no or few blasts, below 5%; 2% blasts fits it. It has an excellent prognosis and accounts for 85-90% of all presentations. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 38, 62"},{"q":"What is the median age at diagnosis of myelodysplastic syndrome?","choices":["15 years","50 years","60 years","70 years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Myelodysplastic syndrome is often idiopathic and occurs in an older population, with a median age of 70 years. It is more common in men and sometimes follows prior chemotherapy or radiation. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 46, 58"},{"q":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia produces increased numbers of which cells?","choices":["Lymphoblasts","Red blood cells","Plasma cells","Granulocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic myelogenous leukemia originates from hematopoietic stem cells but produces increased numbers of granulocytes, which are more mature than blasts. The marrow shows marked granulocytic proliferation and myelocytes in chronic phase. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 56, 60, 61"},{"q":"Which group of symptoms makes up the B-symptoms of a hematologic malignancy?","choices":["Bone pain, anemia, kidney injury","Weight loss, fever, night sweats","Fatigue, pallor, breathlessness","Bruising, petechiae, bleeding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. B-symptoms are weight loss, fever and night sweats, with loss of appetite listed too. They appear in acute lymphoblastic leukemia, acute myeloid leukemia and chronic myelogenous leukemia, and lymphomas often present with night sweats, weight loss and fevers. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 22, 35, 59"},{"q":"For an older patient with acute myeloid leukemia who is unfit for intensive chemotherapy, which treatment is used?","choices":["Hypomethylating agent ± venetoclax","Cytarabine plus anthracycline","Arsenic and retinoic acid","Phlebotomy and hydroxyurea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unfit patients receive low-intensity therapy, frequently a hypomethylating agent with or without venetoclax. Fitness matters most in older patients, and survival is lowest over age 70. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 10, 40, 41, 43, 52"},{"q":"Which cell characterizes Hodgkin lymphoma?","choices":["Promyelocytes","Lymphoblasts","Reed-Sternberg cells","Plasma cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma, about 10% of lymphomas, is characterized by few malignant Reed-Sternberg cells among numerous immune effector cells. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma makes up the other 90% and includes B-cell and T-cell types. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does the fusion protein of chronic myelogenous leukemia do to the cell?","choices":["Adds chromosome copies","Drives growth, blocks apoptosis","Arrests the promyelocyte","Raises cytokines, fibrosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The chromosome 9 and 22 translocation joins two genes, the BCR-ABL1 (breakpoint cluster region-Abelson 1) fusion, making a functional fusion enzyme that promotes cell proliferation and reduces apoptosis: the cell cannot turn off and constantly divides. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How is myelofibrosis often treated?","choices":["Janus kinase inhibitors","Phlebotomy","Tyrosine kinase inhibitors","Arsenic with retinoic acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myelofibrosis is often treated with targeted inhibitors, Janus kinase inhibitors, which are palliative. Patients should be referred to a hematologist upon diagnosis. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Where does normal hematopoiesis begin?","choices":["Splenic tissue","Thymic tissue","Bone marrow stem cells","Lymph node tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normal hematopoiesis begins in the bone marrow with hematopoietic stem cells, which differentiate into myeloid and lymphoid progenitors and then into the terminally differentiated cell lines. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 5, 15, 19"},{"q":"Above what white count does acute myeloid leukemia need emergent leukapheresis?","choices":["30,000","50,000","100,000","200,000"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In acute myeloid leukemia, white counts are often elevated but may be low; unlike acute lymphoblastic leukemia, counts above 200,000 are rare, and every count above 100,000 needs emergent leukapheresis. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 35, 61"},{"q":"Which site may T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia involve, giving it lymphoma-like features?","choices":["A fibrotic marrow","The thymus","The gums","Mainly blood and marrow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia is rarer and usually has lymphoma-like characteristics, and it may involve the thymus. A mediastinal mass may be seen on chest X-ray in T-cell disease. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 19, 22"},{"q":"How is Philadelphia chromosome-positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia treated?","choices":["Kinase inhibitor plus chemotherapy","Arsenic and retinoic acid","Phlebotomy and hydroxyurea","Hypomethylating agent alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Philadelphia chromosome-positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia, carrying the chromosome 9 and 22 translocation, is treated with a tyrosine kinase inhibitor plus chemotherapy. Allogeneic transplant is added where feasible for adverse-risk or relapsed disease. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which acute myeloid leukemia subtypes may show gum hyperplasia or other extramedullary disease?","choices":["Undifferentiated","Myelomonocytic and monocytic","Promyelocytic","Erythroid and megakaryocytic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The myelomonocytic and monocytic subtypes of acute myeloid leukemia tend to have more extramedullary involvement, and they may involve the gums (hyperplasia). — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 33, 35"},{"q":"Which complications are listed for polycythemia vera?","choices":["Central nervous system spread","Thrombosis, splenomegaly, bleeding","Coagulopathy and early death","Portal hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The complications of polycythemia vera are thrombosis, splenomegaly and bleeding. Its prognosis is still excellent, with an estimated survival of 14 years. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which pair of instructions is given for both polycythemia vera and essential thrombocythemia?","choices":["Phlebotomy and hydroxyurea","Janus kinase inhibitor, palliation","Admission for work-up","Hematology referral, stop smoking"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with polycythemia vera and with essential thrombocythemia should be referred to a hematologist and advised to cease smoking. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 29, 52, 53, 54"},{"q":"Which patients with chronic myelogenous leukemia are considered for allogeneic stem cell transplant?","choices":["Young, healthy, kinase-resistant","Any chronic-phase patient","Older, unfit patients","Those in sustained response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Allogeneic stem cell transplant is used in young, healthy patients who have resistance to tyrosine kinase inhibitors or advanced-stage chronic myelogenous leukemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 63"}]};
